<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dreros</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dreros is an ancient Greek city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, near modern Neapoli. It preserves a law inscribed around 650–600 BCE limiting repeated tenure of the office of kosmos, one of the earliest surviving Greek laws on stone, and an early temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-delphinios">Apollo Delphinios</a> with hammered bronze statues. A later oath of its young men pledged hostility to Lyttos and friendship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dreros.jpg" alt="Dreros" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olaf Tausch · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dreros_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies near Neapoli in the regional unit of Lasithi, 16 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agios-nikolaos">Agios Nikolaos</a>. It comprises two acropoleis with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> of the Archaic period between them, a necropolis and traces of fortifications. The city is otherwise known only from a chance remark of the 9th-century Byzantine grammarian Theognostus in his De orthographia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeology shows that Dreros was first settled by mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> early in the Archaic period, at about the same time as Lato and Prinias, and its inscriptions are in the Dorian dialect. The city was most prosperous from the 8th to the 6th century BCE. It later became a minor satellite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and declined so far in Hellenistic times that it was not among the thirty Cretan cities that made a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a>, the Attalid king of Pergamum, in 183 BCE, though it remained inhabited into the Byzantine period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The site was first excavated in 1917, and almost the whole city and its necropolis have since been uncovered. A large communal cistern dug between the late 3rd and early 2nd century BCE contained Archaic inscriptions, among them the sacred law of Dreros, the earliest complete record of constitutional law found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, which names the Dorian Cretan titles kosmos and damios.</p><p>South of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> stands the Delphinion, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-delphinios">Apollo Delphinios</a> and dating from about 750 BCE, one of the earliest free-standing Greek temples; Spyridon Marinatos excavated it in 1935. Three statuettes of bronze sheet hammered over cores, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> and their mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> and known as the Dreros Triad, came from its precinct and are now in the Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Herakleion</a>. In 1936 Pierre Demargne and Henri van Effenterre found two Eteocretan inscriptions on grey schist in the cistern; kept at the museum of Neapolis, they were lost during the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dreros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The young men of <b>Dreros</b> swore an oath, preserved on stone, to be hostile to Lyttos and friendly to Knossos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
